    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-46539-1",
    "abstract": "Water transit times strongly influence water quality, temperature, and seasonal hydrologic response of river systems. How water transit times may shift under future climates remains unconstrained, especially in mountainous regions experiencing rapid snowpack declines. Here, we estimated historical (2006\u20132013) and future (2086\u20132093) water transit times in five headwater catchments within the U.S. Pacific Northwest using sequential precipitation input tagging within the Water Tracer enabled version of the Weather Research and Forecasting Hydrologic model. Our results indicate water transit times are 18% (35\u201364 days) faster on average under the Representative Carbon Pathways (RCP) 8.5 climate scenario due to shifts in rain-snow partitioning, with higher fractions of younger water in the wet season and older water in the dry season. These results suggest shifts in rain-snow partitioning in snowmelt dominated catchments of the Pacific Northwest will shorten water transit times leading to likely impacts on regional water quality, temperature, and hydrologic seasonality.",
    "field": "Hydroclimatology",
    "problem": "How water transit times may shift under future climates remains unconstrained, especially in mountainous regions experiencing rapid snowpack declines, which hinders predictions of future impacts on water quality, temperature, and the seasonal hydrologic response of river systems.",
    "method": "This study uses a sequential precipitation input tagging framework within a water-tracer-enabled hydrologic model to estimate and compare historical and future water transit times, thereby assessing the impact of shifting rain-snow partitioning under climate change.",
    "task": "The task is to estimate historical and future water transit times in headwater catchments of the U.S. Pacific Northwest, using a hydrologic model to understand how shifts in rain-snow partitioning under a future climate scenario will affect them.",
    "model": [
        "WRF",
        "CMIP5",
        "WT-WRF-Hydro"
    ],
